WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.530
ANAGHA DIWANJI: In
this video, we'll

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:05.040
show you how to detect anomalies
using Streaming Analytics

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.060
and AI.

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.980
My name is Anagha Diwanji.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.080
And my colleague
Prerna Bellara and I

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:13.070
are solution engineers
for Google Cloud.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.530
Many companies are dealing
with the proliferation

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.650
of data and difficulty in
making sense of that data

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.470
at the speed required
to make it useful.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.350
This solution aims at sifting
through the mass of data

00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:27.080
to the events that need
additional attention--

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.840
those outliers that indicate
that something is not

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.009
going as expected.

00:00:32.009 --> 00:00:34.150
Now, what is anomaly detection?

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.920
Spotting the unusual,
catching fraud,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.050
and discovering
strange activity.

00:00:39.050 --> 00:00:42.410
All of this sounds like Sherlock
Holmes solving a mystery.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.680
That's what anomaly detection
is, except we may not

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.780
have a clear suspect
to search for,

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.900
and you may not even
know what the crime is.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:54.560
When you see this example,
do you spot any anomalies?

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.800
First thought, it's fairly easy
to see the large spike appears

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.390
to be an outlier.

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:02.180
But what if you have a
large sample of data?

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.780
Now the perception changes, and
what appeared to be an anomaly

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.090
turns out to be part of a
regular and familiar pattern.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:12.260
In this, the anomalous
behavior is not the fact

00:01:12.260 --> 00:01:14.540
that there are spikes
but the observation

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:18.260
that their frequency
fluctuates during an episode.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:20.810
The highlighted region
denotes an anomaly

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.730
because the same
low value exists

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:24.980
for an abnormally long time.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.990
Anomalies are not defined
by their own characteristics

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:30.930
but in contrast
to what is normal.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:34.470
The speed with which businesses
are able to identify anomalies

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.780
impacts their
options for response.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.690
For example, not detecting
a fraudulent financial

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.870
transaction immediately
and instead within hours

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:47.680
or even days often results in
writing off the financial loss.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.190
On the other hand, finding
the anomalous transaction

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.710
in seconds allows
for the invalidation

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.170
of the transaction
and corrective actions

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.700
to prevent future fraud.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:01:59.920
Similarly, by
detecting anomalies

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:02.380
in industrial
equipment, manufacturers

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.530
can predict and prevent
catastrophic failures

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.830
that could cause capital
and human loss by initiating

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:13.330
proactive equipment shutdown
and preventative maintenance.

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:15.580
Likewise for retail,
we can see this

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:18.310
being used to help
manage inventory, detect

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:21.790
fraud, and track user
behavior patterns.

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.490
Another example of an
industry application

00:02:24.490 --> 00:02:27.460
is a telco company doing
subscriber analytics

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:30.580
and specifically focusing on
detecting anonymous wireless

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.420
traffic, real-time fraud
detection on call and SIM,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.610
and user behavior patterns.

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:39.430
And across all these
industries, cybersecurity

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.190
is top of mind for
most customers because

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:45.880
of economic costs of intrusion
and security attacks.

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.410
Let's talk about how to design a
system for intrusion detection.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.870
Let's deep dive into
our story for today.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.050
In our story, we have
Stacy, who's a data analyst.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:58.270
Her main role is to
manage and monitor

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:00.610
the network for a
large corporation.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:03.500
She needs to ensure everything
is running smoothly.

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:06.100
And if there's an outage
or unusual behavior,

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:09.520
she needs to report it and
take corrective action.

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.770
Stacy's company
currently has a hard time

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.170
anticipating network
traffic changes,

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.300
which creates impediments
in identifying

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.070
cybersecurity attacks.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:21.280
They want to catch unusual
behavior and security attacks

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.220
in real time and take action.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:27.500
Leveraging Google Cloud
Streaming service,

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:30.770
Stacy can monitor the
network, analyze the traffic,

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:32.960
and now has opportunity
to review and take

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:36.010
action such as file a
ticket in near real time.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.780
When anomalies are found,
the solution automatically

00:03:38.780 --> 00:03:41.720
sends an alert to Stacy
and updates network health

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:43.310
dashboards.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.000
Now, let's see an
overview of how

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.050
Stacy implemented anomaly
detection in Google Cloud

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.520
environment.

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:51.740
There's four major
components to this--

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.910
ingest, extract and
train, predict, and alert.

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:58.880
The first step is ingesting
unlabeled streaming data

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:00.680
from all the sources.

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.290
Google Cloud Pub/Sub
and Google Cloud Storage

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.120
make it easy to
ingest streaming data.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.890
Next, we transform and store
this data in Google BigQuery,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.920
extract features using
Dataflow, and train the model

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.840
with just some SQL.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.360
After training this
data, we use Dataflow

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.480
to allow for online real-time
reporting and highlighting

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:21.980
outliers.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:25.310
But it isn't just enough to
detect anomalies and monitor

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:26.480
this data.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.270
The key is the action we take.

00:04:29.270 --> 00:04:31.730
BI tools, such as
Tableau and Looker,

00:04:31.730 --> 00:04:34.340
allow you to not only
view this information

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:38.630
but take appropriate action such
as informing the security team.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.550
This was just a brief overview.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.480
Now let's take a closer look at
each of these four components.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:46.850
My colleague Prerna Bellara
will take it forward.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.160
PRERNA BELLARA: Let's assume
I'm Stacy, an analyst tasked

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.370
with solving this business
problem that we discussed

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.228
earlier.

00:04:57.228 --> 00:04:59.020
First, I'll look into
the GitHub repository

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:02.350
to understand all of the
four steps mentioned earlier.

00:05:02.350 --> 00:05:04.490
The first step,
you might recall,

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.130
would be to ingest the data.

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:08.500
Google Cloud's Pub/Sub
topic can help us

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.240
with the streaming ingestion.

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.440
In order to do so, we also need
to simulate production data

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.980
to train our model.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:20.680
The photo on the
right shows the schema

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:24.610
for the model, which
resembles a NetFlow schema.

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:28.690
We notice about 250,000 events
per second in my production

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.970
environment, which
comes up to about 20

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:33.640
terabytes of data from
heterogeneous sources

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:36.580
coming through a Pub/Sub topic,
some of which will be labeled,

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.450
some maybe unlabeled.

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:42.160
Let's look at the next step.

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:43.810
Now that we've set
up the ingestion,

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:45.820
the next question
is, how do we ensure

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:47.410
the solution is scalable?

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:50.300
If you recall, we were
generating or anticipating

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.330
250,000 events per second.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.420
We need to aggregate this data.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:59.440
This ties to the second step,
which is extract and train.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.620
Using Dataflow [? BIM ?] and
its [INAUDIBLE] transform

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:05.380
such as minimum,
maximum, and count,

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:08.920
we can ingest and aggregate
these logs or events,

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.440
thereby reducing the
number of elements

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:14.520
or the amount or volume of
elements from 11 gigabytes

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:19.210
to 1.03 gigabytes,
as you can see.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:24.250
Additionally, Dataflow [? BIM ?]
allows us to autoscale, thereby

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:26.800
generating or helping
us build a pipeline that

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:27.760
is production ready.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:35.220
We will use [? BQMS ?]
k-means clustering model

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:37.660
to train this extracted data.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.880
We've decided to
use k-means as a--

00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.260
since it's a common algorithm
used for unsupervised machine

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:45.460
learning.

00:06:45.460 --> 00:06:49.190
As you can see, I wrote the
SQL query to create the model,

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:52.780
ran the command, and in 15
minutes we have a model.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.580
Configuration is easy.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:56.920
Simply mention the columns
I wanted as features

00:06:56.920 --> 00:06:59.120
and set the number of clusters.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:01.750
This is great, because machine
learning requires some trial

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.640
and error, and the
quick turnaround time

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:05.680
helps me to play with
the configuration

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.430
and determine the most optimal
combination for my use case.

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:15.280
Another great feature for an
analyst is the scheduled query.

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:18.340
I don't want to just create a
model one time and leave it.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.340
I want it to learn continuously
and be updated with the latest

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.390
patterns.

00:07:22.390 --> 00:07:25.240
Using a scheduled query and
a [? stored ?] procedure,

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:28.060
I can rerun this query
in a timely manner,

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.025
thereby keeping
my model updated.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.300
Additionally, once
the module is created

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.440
we will calculate the normalized
distance from each centroid

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:41.830
or grouping to determine if
the input that we received

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.830
is too far from the
cluster, thereby deeming it

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:45.530
as an outlier.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:54.360
Now that the model is ready,
it's time to detect outliers.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:55.350
How do we do this?

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.300
The answer is Dataflow.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:00.420
We pass the normalized data
in the pipeline as an input

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:03.480
and reuse this data to
calculate how far our input

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:04.850
data is from the center.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:08.160
If it meets a
distance threshold,

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.380
we don't throw them
as an outlier--

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.050
we store them as an outlier
in the BigQuery table.

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:15.960
The result, we are now
notified of the outlier

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:17.780
and can provide
little to no downtime.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.810
Now that we have enabled
real-time detection,

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:28.060
it's time to view our
results, and most importantly,

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:29.880
take appropriate action.

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.309
We will leverage Google's
Looker Dashboard to do this.

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:34.919
Let's take a look.

00:08:34.919 --> 00:08:37.799
As you can see, in
this Looker Dashboard,

00:08:37.799 --> 00:08:41.130
I can see the number
of events, the source,

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:45.370
whether it was an anomaly,
as well as the bytes.

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:47.860
There is another column
over here called Actions,

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.390
which allows me to take
appropriate action.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:53.250
I can either create a Jira
ticket, text a security

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:57.120
consultant, or email the
security team, thereby

00:08:57.120 --> 00:09:01.250
notifying my team
of the right action.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:04.360
You can also use any
other third-party tool

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.810
that is supported by Google
Cloud for your reporting needs.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.670
In summary, we've built this
in a short time, processed

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:18.360
over 250,000 events
using Dataflow.

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:23.010
We can consume all of this
20 terabytes of daily data

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.740
from data sources.

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:28.410
We trained and evaluated
1 terabyte data

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:31.480
in 15 minutes using SQL.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.810
We found outliers
in near real time

00:09:33.810 --> 00:09:36.690
and took appropriate action.

00:09:36.690 --> 00:09:40.110
We've covered a lot of ground.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.230
For more information
on how to implement

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:46.070
this in your environment, please
check out the GitHub link.

